<Base title="About" description="Who I am and what I work on.">
  <h1>About</h1>

  <p>I'm an applied economist. PhD from Georgia Tech in 2024, with a thesis on network centrality, spatial heterogeneity, and optimal transport in international trade. My research is in the family of "applied causal inference with a spatial conscience." That is: I care a lot about identification, I care equally about whether the result generalises to the geography it claims to describe, and I tend to study programs where capital moves through pass-through intermediaries (Community Development Entities, mutual guarantee societies, microfinance institutions, central-bank refinancing windows) rather than directly to final beneficiaries.</p>

  <p>The thing I'm best at is taking a policy question that looks like a market-structure problem ("rural communities can't access credit") and showing it's actually an intermediary-allocation problem ("the public capital we routed through these particular CDEs went where the CDEs preferred to deploy it"). That reframing matters because the fixes are different and the cheap fix is usually the second one.</p>

  <h2>What I work on</h2>

  <p>Three threads, in rough order of how much time they take:</p>

  <p><strong>Research.</strong> Right now the active papers are (1) <em>The rural mobilization gap in U.S. place-based tax credit</em>, which decomposes the NMTC rural penalty into between-CDE and within-CDE components; (2) <em>Trade in the spotlight</em> with Dr. Elizaveta Gonchar, which applies an effective-distance metric to bilateral economic exposure under sanctions; and (3) a Portugal/Ireland extension of the NMTC methodology to EU blended-finance instruments, in collaboration with Katia Antunes. The larger arc is the <strong>Spatial Public-Finance Atlas</strong>, a multi-program atlas of how public-subsidy capital flows across geography.</p>

  <p><strong>Teaching.</strong> I run three teaching sites: <a href="https://ihelfrich.github.io/inference-lab/">Inference Lab</a> for applied causal methods, <a href="https://ihelfrich.github.io/macroprep/">Macro Prep</a> for intermediate macroeconomics, and <a href="https://github.com/ihelfrich/pictures-of-inference">Pictures of Inference</a> for a visual textbook on statistical inference (in draft). I tutor graduate students and advanced undergraduates in econometrics, finance, and economic theory. I take new clients selectively, with priority on people working on applied-causal projects in development or blended finance.</p>

  <p><strong>Tools.</strong> I build datasets and viewers as part of the research workflow rather than as portfolio pieces. The <a href="https://ihelfrich.github.io/us-nmtc-viewer/">US NMTC Viewer</a> is the data spine behind the rural-mobilization paper. The EffDist V2026 dataset is a global gridded effective-distance product co-developed with Dr. Gonchar. All of it lives on GitHub and is CC-BY-SA or CC-BY licensed unless otherwise noted.</p>

  <h2>What I don't do</h2>

  <p>I don't take tutoring clients whose objective is to outsource problem sets. I don't co-author papers I haven't substantively contributed to. I don't ghost-write. I don't accept consulting work where the deliverable is a foregone conclusion. And I don't currently take new institutional consulting engagements unless they overlap meaningfully with the existing research program (DFI-funded impact evaluation in rural finance is the main exception).</p>

  <h2>Background</h2>

  <p>I worked in signal processing before economics, and have a master's in GIS. The signal-processing background shows up in the spatial-methods work; the GIS shows up in how I think about geographic-scale aggregation. Both feel relevant to the kinds of questions I now study.</p>

  <p>I live in Atlanta. I'm engaged to Dr. Elizaveta Gonchar, who is also my most frequent research collaborator. We work on the spatial economics of capital, externalities, and trade together.</p>

